WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.675
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.675 --> 00:00:05.690
we help some fresh developers
find their ideal VM

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:06.890
configuration.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.380
Will we fix things before
the performance goes stale?

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.400
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:17.482
Enfuego coin is a hot new
cryptocurrency trading startup

00:00:17.482 --> 00:00:19.190
that has just taken
the plunge into using

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.530
custom images for their VMs.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:23.600
Sadly, though, after
months of trying,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.670
they couldn't get the
right machine type--

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.770
too high, too low, costs
all over the place.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.260
But they are not alone.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:31.820
Without the right
level of analysis,

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.424
profiling, and
benchmarking, it's

00:00:33.424 --> 00:00:35.090
really difficult to
figure out if you're

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:36.860
paying too much for
your configuration

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.890
or are throttling your
performance unknowingly.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.740
As it turns out, hitting the
sweet spot is really hard

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.840
and involves doing
something like this.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.080
Firstly, discuss what your
expected performance benchmarks

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.500
are for various systems from
the perspective of the clients

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.740
and the other key dependencies,
i.e., the systems that

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:55.860
will be using it.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.220
Number two, create a
benchmark and testing suite

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.550
to test I/O performance,
CPU utilization, and memory

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.200
performance for your VM.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.600
Then, number three, start
with a small machine,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.570
benchmark it, and test the
results against those desired

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:09.560
outcomes.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:10.970
And if things
aren't fast enough,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.440
try the next largest machine.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.020
And then finally, continue
this entire process

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.240
until you've found
the sweet spot.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.250
Now listen, you've
got better things

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.770
to do than worry about
this problem, which

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.780
is why Google
Compute Engine does

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.470
all the heavy lifting for you.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.890
In fact, it will just
tell you directly

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.680
if you could be saving
money on your VM page

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.620
and what configuration
it suggests using.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.200
So how does GCE come up
with these recommendations?

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.320
Compute Engine monitors the
CPU and memory utilization

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.140
of running virtual machines
and makes recommendations

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.260
using the last eight days of
data on standard and custom

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:45.967
machine types.

00:01:45.967 --> 00:01:48.050
There's a lot of magic
going on here that we don't

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.790
have time to cover, though.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:52.970
So to learn more about how
to use the recommendations,

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.090
and how Stackdriver can help
you get even better estimates,

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.340
check out the article linked
in the description below.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.464
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:02:00.464 --> 00:02:02.240
Performance Atlas
content because when

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.490
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.240
